Sykes Seafood

Headline description

Sykes Seafood is an independent seafood processor and distributor supplying retail, foodservice and manufacturing customers across the UK and Europe.

Business Overview

* Founded in Manchester in 1862 and now headquartered in Warrington, Sykes specialises in warm-water and cold-water prawns alongside a broader seafood portfolio. [1]
* Alan Dale privately controls the business through ADRD Investments, whose subsidiaries include Sykes Seafood and Dutch shrimp processor Klaas Puul. [2]
* The business offers more than 600 products across 110 species, supplying branded and customer own-label seafood to retailers, wholesalers, foodservice operators and manufacturers. [1]
* Its Liverpool facility processes approximately 350 tonnes of seafood per week, while Klaas Puul provides additional processing capacity in Edam and Volendam and shrimp-peeling operations in Morocco. [3]
* Sykes markets its premium range under the Arctic Royal brand, complemented by the Clear Seas range, with both brands serving retail and foodservice customers alongside the business’s own-label offering. [1]

Recent Developments and M&A Activity

* Between 2020 and 2023, the group acquired Klaas Puul, Ruskim Seafoods and the trading assets of The Big Prawn Co., expanding its European shrimp-processing operations and UK distribution capabilities. [4]
* In 2023, the group opened its 167,000 sq ft head office and distribution centre in Warrington and transferred Big Prawn’s production from Norfolk to Liverpool. [2,3]
* In FY25, operating profit recovered to £12.9m from £4.6m in FY24, while revenue remained broadly flat following the integration of the acquired businesses. [2]

Key Financial Information

* ADRD Investments’ consolidated revenue declined by 0.7% to £347.4m in FY25, while operating profit increased to £12.9m and the operating margin improved from 1.3% to 3.7%. Profit before tax reached £8.5m, compared with a £0.9m loss in FY24. [2]
* These figures cover the consolidated parent group. Separately, the UK trading company, Sykes Seafood Limited, reported FY25 revenue of £241.7m and profit before tax of £3.8m. [5]

Select Product Overview

* Cold-water prawns: The range includes cooked and peeled prawns.
* Warm-water prawns: King prawns are available in a variety of sizes and formats.
* Argentinian prawns: Wild red prawns are available whole or peeled.
* Whitefish: The portfolio includes cod, haddock, pollock, basa and hake.
* Added-value prawns: The range includes marinated prawns and breaded, battered and tempura-coated products.
* Squid and octopus: The business supplies a range of frozen cephalopod products. [1]

Lyons & Farne

Headline description

Lyons and Farne comprise Labeyrie’s UK seafood operations, supplying prawns, speciality shellfish and salmon primarily under retailers’ own labels.

Business Overview

* Labeyrie Fine Foods operates through two UK businesses: Lyons Seafoods in Warminster, Wiltshire, and Farne Salmon & Trout in Duns, Scotland. [1]
* Both businesses joined the group in 2004. Labeyrie is jointly owned by PAI Partners and agricultural cooperative Lur Berri, which each hold 46%, with employees holding the remaining equity. [1,2]
* Lyons supplies prawns and speciality seafood to UK retail, catering and manufacturing customers, with a portfolio spanning warm-water and cold-water prawns alongside other shellfish. [3]
* Farne specialises in fresh and smoked salmon, producing cold-smoked, hot-smoked, ready-to-eat and natural salmon products for cooking at home. [4]
* Supply to major retailers under their own labels forms the core of the UK offering, complemented by The Real Seafood Co. brand, which continued to grow with retail customers in FY25. [1,3]

Recent Developments and M&A Activity

* In October 2025, trade press reported that Houlihan Lokey was marketing Lyons and Farne together, citing approximately £30m of EBITDA and an asking valuation of £240–300m. These represented reported sale-process figures rather than an agreed transaction value. [5]
* In FY25, Lyons’ revenue increased by 8.3%, while Farne’s revenue declined by 1.0%. Both businesses nevertheless increased operating profit, taking their combined EBIT to £23.2m. [6]
* In August and September 2026, press reports indicated plans for a wider sale of Labeyrie by July 2027, separate from the previously reported disposal process for its UK businesses. [7]

Key Financial Information

* For the year ended 30 June 2025, Lyons generated revenue of £130.3m and operating profit of £11.8m, compared with £120.3m and £8.6m respectively in FY24. Farne generated revenue of £117.9m and operating profit of £11.4m, compared with £119.0m and £9.4m respectively in FY24. [6]
* Together, the businesses generated revenue of £248.2m and EBIT of £23.2m, equivalent to a 9.3% operating margin, compared with £239.3m, £18.0m and 7.5% respectively in FY24. These figures represent a simple aggregation before intercompany eliminations or carve-out adjustments. [6]
* FY25 profit before tax was £13.4m for Lyons and £11.9m for Farne. [6]

Select Product Overview

* Lyons prawns and shellfish: The range provides seafood ingredients for home cooking.
* Lyons seafood cocktails: The offering includes prepared prawn cocktails.
* Lyons seaside favourites: The range includes convenient prepared seafood products.
* Farne cold-smoked salmon: Options include mild oak-smoked, hickory-smoked and organic salmon.
* Farne hot-smoked salmon: The range includes lemon-and-parsley salmon alongside other flavoured fillets.
* Farne natural salmon: The offering includes salmon products prepared for cooking at home. [3,4]

Iberconsa

Headline description

Iberconsa is a Platinum Equity-owned, vertically integrated frozen seafood group with fishing operations in Argentina and Southern Africa.

Business Overview

* Founded in 1981 and headquartered in Vigo, Spain, Iberconsa operates across the seafood value chain, from wild catch and processing through to cold storage, distribution and sales. [1]
* Platinum Equity acquired the business in June 2019 from Portobello Capital and founding-family shareholders and continues to hold it within its portfolio. [2]
* According to Platinum Equity, the group operates 40 vessels, five processing plants and four cold-storage distribution facilities, supplying customers in more than 60 countries. [2]
* Its fleet operates primarily in Argentina, Namibia and South Africa, with approximately 90% of sales volumes produced in-house, giving the group substantial control over its supply. [2,3]
* Iberconsa supplies trading, retail and foodservice customers across Europe and Asia-Pacific, with a portfolio centred on hake, wild Argentine red shrimp and squid. [1,3]

Recent Developments and M&A Activity

* In 2024, the group established Iberconsa (Shanghai) Trading, strengthening its commercial presence in China. [1]
* In May 2026, trade press reported that Platinum Equity intended to retain the business, with renewed emphasis on growth and potential acquisitions. [4]
* In September 2026, trade press reported that Iberconsa had completed its corporate debt refinancing, following earlier concerns about debt maturities falling due in 2027. [5]

Key Financial Information

* The Spanish reporting entity, Grupo Ibérica de Congelados, reported FY25 revenue of €398.1m, compared with €419.3m in FY24. [6]
* For the wider Bering rating-group perimeter, S&P reported adjusted EBITDA of €75.4m in FY25, compared with approximately €69.0m in FY24. Separately, Fitch reported FY25 adjusted EBITDA of €82.0m and an adjusted EBITDA margin of 18.2%. [3,7]
* The Spanish reporting-entity revenue and the credit agencies’ adjusted EBITDA figures use different reporting scopes and definitions and should therefore be considered separately. [3,6,7]

Select Product Overview

* Hake: The range includes frozen fillets, medallions and steaks supplied in retail bags and boxes.
* Argentine red shrimp: Wild shrimp are available in display boxes and retail trays.
* Cephalopods: The portfolio includes squid and octopus products supplied in formats such as frozen blocks and skin packs. [1]
